Falsifies `pci::verification::intx_interrupt_number_is_total_and_bounded`.

The defect: the pin is decremented without saturating, `pin as u32 - 1` in place of
`(pin as u32).saturating_sub(1)`. This is the defect the property was written against rather
than an invented one: the harness's own doc comment names "the pin-0 case that used to panic in
debug builds", and every caller checks `pin == 0` first, so the guard reads as dead code to
anyone tidying the expression.

Expected to fail: the totality of the function, at `pin == 0`, which Kani reports as an
arithmetic overflow on the subtraction rather than through either assertion. A device that
reports interrupt pin 0 is a device the kernel meets during enumeration of hardware nobody
vetted.

diff --git a/crates/pci/src/lib.rs b/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/pci/src/lib.rs
@@ -648,3 +648,3 @@ pub fn intx_interrupt_number(base: u32, dev: u8, pin: u8) -> u32 {
     // check pin == 0 before calling; this is defence in depth, not an invitation.
-    base.saturating_add((dev as u32 + (pin as u32).saturating_sub(1)) % 4)
+    base.saturating_add((dev as u32 + (pin as u32 - 1)) % 4)
 }
